<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-injections-surjections" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>One-to-One and Onto Transformations</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Two questions can be asked of any transformation: does it ever send two different
      inputs to the same output, and does it reach every possible output? For matrix
      transformations both questions reduce to facts about the matrix that we already know
      how to check.
    </p>
    <p>
      Several figures in this section are interactive: you can click and drag the heads
      of the vectors to explore the transformation. They are powered by the demo
      framework from
      <pubtitle>Interactive Linear Algebra</pubtitle> by Margalit and Rabinoff
      (<url href="https://github.com/QBobWatson/ila" visual="github.com/QBobWatson/ila"/>),
      used under the GPL.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-injections">
    <title>One-to-One Transformations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-injective">
      <title>Injective (One-to-One) Linear Transformation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^n \to \R^m</m> be a linear transformation, and let
          <m>\vec{x}_1</m> and <m>\vec{x}_2</m> be in <m>\R^n</m>. We say that <m>T</m>
          is an <term>injection</term> or is <term>one-to-one</term> if
          <m>\vec{x}_1 \neq \vec{x}_2</m> implies
          <m>T(\vec{x}_1) \neq T(\vec{x}_2)</m>. Equivalently, if
          <m>T(\vec{x}_1) = T(\vec{x}_2)</m>, then <m>\vec{x}_1 = \vec{x}_2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <figure xml:id="fig-injection-schematic">
      <caption>
        Which transformation is an injection? On the left, two different inputs share an
        output, so the transformation is not one-to-one. On the right, distinct inputs
        have distinct outputs.
      </caption>
      <image width="82%">
        <prefigure label="pf-injection-schematic" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
          <diagram dimensions="(520,220)" margins="8">
            <coordinates bbox="(0,0,10.4,4.4)">
              <ellipse center="(1.3,2.2)" axes="(1.0,1.9)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
              <ellipse center="(4.2,2.2)" axes="(1.0,1.9)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
              <point p="(1.3,3.1)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <point p="(1.3,2.2)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <point p="(1.3,1.3)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <point p="(4.2,3.0)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <point p="(4.2,1.2)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <line endpoints="((1.5,3.1),(4.0,3.0))" stroke="red" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
              <line endpoints="((1.5,2.2),(4.0,1.2))" stroke="red" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
              <line endpoints="((1.5,1.3),(4.0,3.0))" stroke="red" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
              <ellipse center="(6.6,2.2)" axes="(1.0,1.9)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
              <ellipse center="(9.5,2.2)" axes="(1.0,1.9)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
              <point p="(6.6,3.0)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <point p="(6.6,1.3)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <point p="(9.5,3.1)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <point p="(9.5,2.2)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <point p="(9.5,1.2)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <line endpoints="((6.8,3.0),(9.3,3.1))" stroke="red" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
              <line endpoints="((6.8,1.3),(9.3,1.2))" stroke="red" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
              <label p="(2.75,0.15)" alignment="n"><m>\text{not one-to-one}</m></label>
              <label p="(8.05,0.15)" alignment="n"><m>\text{one-to-one}</m></label>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-injective-iff-trivial">
      <title>One-to-One and the Trivial Solution</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>m \times n</m> matrix and let <m>\vec{x}</m> be a vector
          of length <m>n</m>. Then the transformation induced by <m>A</m>, <m>T_A</m>, is
          one-to-one (1) if and only if <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> implies
          <m>\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> (2).
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          <term>(1) <m>\Longrightarrow</m> (2).</term>
          Let <m>\vec{u}</m> be an element of <m>\R^n</m> such that
          <m>T_A(\vec{u}) = \vec{0}</m>. Then, since <m>T_A</m> is a one-to-one linear
          transformation, we have <m>T_A(\vec{0}) = \vec{0}</m> and hence
          <m>\vec{0} = \vec{u}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(2) <m>\Longrightarrow</m> (1).</term>
          Let <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> be elements in <m>\R^n</m> such that
          <m>A\vec{u} = A\vec{v}</m>. Then <m>A(\vec{u} - \vec{v}) = \vec{0}</m>, which
          implies <m>\vec{u} - \vec{v} = \vec{0}</m>, or <m>\vec{u} = \vec{v}</m>. Hence
          we have shown that if <m>T_A(\vec{u}) = T_A(\vec{v})</m> then
          <m>\vec{u} = \vec{v}</m>, which means <m>T_A</m> is one-to-one.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <example xml:id="eg-injective-true-false">
      <title>True or False?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, where <m>A</m> is an <m>m \times n</m> matrix.
          If we also assume that <m>T</m> is injective, then which statement is true and
          which one is false?
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                For every <m>\vec{b} \in \R^m</m>, there exists one solution for the
                equation <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p><m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has only the trivial solution.</p></li>
            <li><p>The columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(I) False.</term> For example, let
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
          <m>T : \R^2 \to \R^3</m>, which is a one-to-one transformation. Now, for
          <m>\vec{b} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>, the system
          <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> has no solution, since the augmented matrix is
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 3
            \end{array} \right]
            \xrightarrow{r_3 - r_1}
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 2
            \end{array} \right],
          </md>
          and therefore the system is inconsistent.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(II) True.</term> See
          <xref ref="thm-injective-iff-trivial" text="type-global" />.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(III) True.</term> Note that
          <md>
            A\vec{x} = \vec{0}
            \iff x_1\vec{A}_1 + x_2\vec{A}_2 + \cdots + x_n\vec{A}_n = \vec{0}.
          </md>
          If <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has only the trivial solution, it means that none
          of the columns of <m>A</m> can be written as a linear combination of the rest of
          them, since if for example <m>\vec{A}_1</m> could be written as a linear
          combination
          <md>
            \vec{A}_1 = a_2\vec{A}_2 + a_3\vec{A}_3 + \cdots + a_n\vec{A}_n,
          </md>
          this gives
          <m>\vec{A}_1 - a_2\vec{A}_2 - a_3\vec{A}_3 - \cdots - a_n\vec{A}_n = \vec{0}</m>,
          which contradicts the fact that <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has only the trivial
          solution.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-which-are-injections">
      <title>Which Transformation Is an Injection?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Reflection in the <m>x</m>-axis in <m>\R^2</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Projection into the <m>x</m>-axis in <m>\R^2</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>T \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
                = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(A)</term> The corresponding matrix is
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</m> and the system
          <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has only the trivial solution, hence this is an
          injection.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(B)</term> Similarly,
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>, whereas
          <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has non-trivial solutions, hence this is NOT an
          injection.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(C)</term> Again
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
          \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}</m>; however,
          <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has a free parameter and therefore (C) is not an
          injection. For example,
          <md>
            T \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
            = T \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-injective-geometric">
      <title>A One-to-One Transformation, Seen Geometrically</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^2 \to \R^3</m> be the matrix transformation
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, where
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Is <m>T</m> one-to-one?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Row reducing <m>A</m> gives
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \xrightarrow{r_2 - r_1}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \xrightarrow{r_3 - r_2}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          so there is a pivot in every column. Hence <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has only
          the trivial solution, and <m>T</m> is one-to-one by
          <xref ref="thm-injective-iff-trivial" text="type-global"/>.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-injective-geometric">
          <caption>
            The transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> for
            <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
            Drag the input vector <m>\vec{x}</m> on the left: different inputs always
            produce different outputs <m>\vec{b} = T(\vec{x})</m> on the right, so
            <m>T</m> is one-to-one. The violet dot on the left is the solution set of
            <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m>: it is a single point.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-injective-geometric" iframe="demos/Axequalsb.html?mat=1,0:1,1:0,1&amp;range2=5&amp;closed=true&amp;show=true" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-not-injective-geometric">
      <title>A Transformation That Is Not One-to-One, Seen Geometrically</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^3 \to \R^2</m> be the matrix transformation
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, where
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Is <m>T</m> one-to-one?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The matrix <m>A</m> is already in reduced row echelon form, and the third
          column has no pivot. Solving <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> with free variable
          <m>z</m> gives <m>x = -z</m> and <m>y = -z</m>, so the solution set is
          <md>
            \vec{x} = z \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad z \text{ arbitrary}.
          </md>
          Taking <m>z = 1</m> produces the nontrivial solution
          <md>
            T \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
              \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
            = T \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          so two different inputs share the output <m>\vec{0}</m>, and <m>T</m> is not
          one-to-one.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-not-injective-geometric">
          <caption>
            The transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> for
            <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
            The violet line on the left is the solution set of <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{0}</m>,
            namely all multiples of <m>(-1,-1,1)</m>. Drag <m>\vec{x}</m> along this
            line: the output <m>T(\vec{x})</m> stays at <m>\vec{0}</m>, so <m>T</m>
            sends infinitely many inputs to the same output.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-not-injective-geometric" iframe="demos/Axequalsb.html?mat=1,0,1:0,1,1&amp;range2=5&amp;closed=true&amp;show=true&amp;lock=true&amp;x=0,0,0" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rem-wide-never-injective">
      <title>Wide Matrices Are Never One-to-One</title>
      <p>
        If <m>A</m> is an <m>m \times n</m> matrix with more columns than rows
        (<m>n \gt m</m>), then <m>A</m> cannot have a pivot in every column: there is
        at most one pivot per row, and there are fewer rows than columns. Hence
        <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has a free variable, and the transformation
        <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is <em>never</em> one-to-one. This is why the
        transformation <m>T : \R^3 \to \R^2</m> in the previous example had no chance:
        a transformation from a bigger space to a smaller space must collapse
        different inputs together.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-surjections">
    <title>Onto Transformations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-surjective">
      <title>Surjection (Onto)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^n \to \R^m</m> be a linear transformation. We say that <m>T</m>
          is a <term>surjection</term> or <term>onto</term> if, for every
          <m>\vec{b} \in \R^m</m>, there exists an <m>\vec{x} \in \R^n</m> such that
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <figure xml:id="fig-surjection-schematic">
      <caption>
        Which transformation is a surjection? On the left every element of the codomain is
        hit; on the right one element is missed, so the transformation is not onto.
      </caption>
      <image width="82%">
        <prefigure label="pf-surjection-schematic" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
          <diagram dimensions="(520,220)" margins="8">
            <coordinates bbox="(0,0,10.4,4.4)">
              <ellipse center="(1.3,2.2)" axes="(1.0,1.9)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
              <ellipse center="(4.2,2.2)" axes="(1.0,1.9)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
              <point p="(1.3,3.1)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <point p="(1.3,2.2)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <point p="(1.3,1.3)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <point p="(4.2,3.0)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <point p="(4.2,1.2)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <line endpoints="((1.5,3.1),(4.0,3.0))" stroke="red" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
              <line endpoints="((1.5,2.2),(4.0,1.2))" stroke="red" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
              <line endpoints="((1.5,1.3),(4.0,3.0))" stroke="red" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
              <ellipse center="(6.6,2.2)" axes="(1.0,1.9)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
              <ellipse center="(9.5,2.2)" axes="(1.0,1.9)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
              <point p="(6.6,3.0)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <point p="(6.6,1.3)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <point p="(9.5,3.1)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <point p="(9.5,2.2)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <point p="(9.5,1.2)" fill="blue" size="4"/>
              <line endpoints="((6.8,3.0),(9.3,3.1))" stroke="red" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
              <line endpoints="((6.8,1.3),(9.3,1.2))" stroke="red" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
              <label p="(2.75,0.15)" alignment="n"><m>\text{onto}</m></label>
              <label p="(8.05,0.15)" alignment="n"><m>\text{not onto}</m></label>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="eg-surjection-simple">
      <title>A Transformation That Is Not Onto</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^2 \to \R^2</m> be the linear transformation defined by
          <m>T \begin{bmatrix} a \\ b \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} a + b \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> for all
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} a \\ b \end{bmatrix} \in \R^2</m>. Determine whether <m>T</m>
          is a surjective transformation or not.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Every image has second entry <m>0</m>, so no vector with a nonzero second entry
          is in the range. For instance, <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> is
          never an image. Hence <m>T</m> is not a surjection.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-which-are-surjections">
      <title>Which Transformation Is a Surjection?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>T : \R^3 \to \R^2</m> with
                <m>T(\vec{x}) = \begin{bmatrix} x - z \\ y + z \end{bmatrix}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, where
                <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(I)</term> The matrix is
          <md>
            A = \begin{bmatrix} T(\vec{e}_1) \amp T(\vec{e}_2) \amp T(\vec{e}_3) \end{bmatrix}
              = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          The system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>, or
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          will always have a solution, since
          <m>\left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp b_1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp b_2 \end{array} \right]</m>
          has two leading ones and three variables, one of which is a free variable.
          Hence <m>T</m> is a surjection.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(II)</term> The system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>, or
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
              1 \amp 0 \amp b_1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp b_2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp b_3
            \end{array} \right],
          </md>
          has no solution for <m>b_3 \neq 0</m>, which means <m>T</m> is not a surjection.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-surjective-geometric">
      <title>An Onto Transformation, Seen Geometrically</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^3 \to \R^2</m> be the matrix transformation
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, where
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Is <m>T</m> onto?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The matrix <m>A</m> is already in reduced row echelon form and has a pivot in
          every row. Therefore the augmented matrix of <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> can
          never have a pivot in its last column, and the system is consistent for every
          <m>\vec{b} \in \R^2</m>. Concretely, for any
          <m>\vec{b} = \begin{bmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \end{bmatrix}</m>, the input
          <m>\vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> satisfies
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m>. Hence <m>T</m> is onto.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-surjective-geometric">
          <caption>
            The transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> for
            <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
            Drag the output vector <m>\vec{b}</m> anywhere on the right: the demo
            always finds an input <m>\vec{x}</m> with <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m>,
            because every vector in <m>\R^2</m> is an output of <m>T</m>.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-surjective-geometric" iframe="demos/Axequalsb.html?mat=1,0,1:0,1,1&amp;range2=5&amp;closed=true&amp;show=false&amp;x=1,1,.5" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-not-surjective-geometric">
      <title>A Transformation That Is Not Onto, Seen Geometrically</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^2 \to \R^3</m> be the matrix transformation
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, where
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Is <m>T</m> onto?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Every output of <m>T</m> is a linear combination of the two columns of
          <m>A</m>:
          <md>
            T \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}
            = x \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
            + y \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ x + y \\ y \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          The middle entry of every output is the sum of the other two, so the range of
          <m>T</m> is the plane <m>b_2 = b_1 + b_3</m> in <m>\R^3</m> <mdash/> a
          two-dimensional slice of the three-dimensional codomain. For instance,
          <m>\vec{b} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> has
          <m>1 \neq 1 + 1</m>, so it is not an output of <m>T</m>, and <m>T</m> is
          not onto. Alternatively: <m>A</m> has only two pivots, so it cannot have a
          pivot in every one of its three rows.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-not-surjective-geometric">
          <caption>
            The transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> for
            <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
            The range of <m>T</m> is the violet plane on the right, which is smaller
            than the codomain <m>\R^3</m>. Drag <m>\vec{b}</m> off the violet plane:
            the equation <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m> becomes inconsistent, so such a
            <m>\vec{b}</m> is not an output of <m>T</m>.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-not-surjective-geometric" iframe="demos/Axequalsb.html?mat=1,0:1,1:0,1&amp;range2=5&amp;closed=true&amp;show=false" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rem-tall-never-surjective">
      <title>Tall Matrices Are Never Onto</title>
      <p>
        If <m>A</m> is an <m>m \times n</m> matrix with more rows than columns
        (<m>m \gt n</m>), then <m>A</m> cannot have a pivot in every row: there is at
        most one pivot per column, and there are fewer columns than rows. Hence the
        transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is <em>never</em> onto. This is
        the mirror image of <xref ref="rem-wide-never-injective" text="type-global"/>:
        a transformation from a smaller space into a bigger space cannot fill up its
        codomain.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-injective-surjective-together">
    <title>One-to-One and Onto Together</title>

    <p>
      Both properties are read off from the pivots of <m>A</m>:
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>T</m> is <em>one-to-one</em> if and only if <m>A</m> has a pivot in
            every <em>column</em>, that is, if and only if the columns of <m>A</m> are
            linearly independent.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>T</m> is <em>onto</em> if and only if <m>A</m> has a pivot in every
            <em>row</em>, that is, if and only if the columns of <m>A</m> span
            <m>\R^m</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
      A transformation can have either property without the other, both, or neither.
      The two examples below complete the picture.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="eg-neither-injective-nor-surjective">
      <title>A Transformation That Is Neither One-to-One Nor Onto</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^3 \to \R^2</m> be the matrix transformation
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, where
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\ 2 \amp 4 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Show that <m>T</m> is neither one-to-one nor onto.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Row reducing,
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\ 2 \amp 4 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}
            \xrightarrow{r_2 - 2r_1}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          so <m>A</m> has a single pivot. There is no pivot in the second or third
          column, so <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has two free variables and <m>T</m> is
          not one-to-one: the solution set of <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{0}</m> is the entire
          plane <m>x + 2y - z = 0</m>. There is also no pivot in the second row, so
          <m>T</m> is not onto: the range is
          <m>\Span\left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</m>, a line in
          <m>\R^2</m>, since every column of <m>A</m> is a multiple of
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>. For example,
          <m>\vec{b} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> is not on this line, so
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m> has no solution.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-neither-geometric">
          <caption>
            The transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> for
            <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\ 2 \amp 4 \amp -2 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
            The violet plane on the left is the solution set of
            <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m> for the current <m>\vec{b}</m>; since it
            contains more than one point, <m>T</m> is not one-to-one. The violet line
            on the right is the range of <m>T</m>; since it is smaller than
            <m>\R^2</m>, <m>T</m> is not onto. Drag <m>\vec{b}</m> off the line to
            make the equation inconsistent.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-neither-geometric" iframe="demos/Axequalsb.html?mat=1,2,-1:2,4,-2&amp;range2=5&amp;closed=true&amp;show=true&amp;x=1,1,1" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-both-injective-and-surjective">
      <title>A Transformation That Is Both One-to-One and Onto</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^2 \to \R^2</m> be the matrix transformation
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, where
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Show that <m>T</m> is both one-to-one and onto.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Row reducing,
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \xrightarrow{r_1 \leftrightarrow r_2}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \xrightarrow{r_2 - 2r_1}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}
            \xrightarrow[r_1 + r_2]{-r_2}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          so <m>A</m> has a pivot in every column (<m>T</m> is one-to-one) and in every
          row (<m>T</m> is onto). In fact, for every <m>\vec{b} \in \R^2</m> the
          equation <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m> has <em>exactly one</em> solution,
          <md>
            \vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix} \vec{b},
          </md>
          as can be checked by multiplying the two matrices. (We will study such
          <em>invertible</em> matrices in detail later.)
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-both-geometric">
          <caption>
            The transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> for
            <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
            Drag either vector: every output <m>\vec{b}</m> comes from exactly one
            input <m>\vec{x}</m>, so <m>T</m> is one-to-one and onto. The violet
            region on the right is the range of <m>T</m>, which is all of <m>\R^2</m>.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-both-geometric" iframe="demos/Axequalsb.html?mat=2,1:1,1&amp;range2=5&amp;closed=true&amp;show=true&amp;x=1,1" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rem-square-injective-iff-surjective">
      <title>Square Matrices: One-to-One Equals Onto</title>
      <p>
        For an <m>n \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>, having a pivot in every column is the
        same as having <m>n</m> pivots, which is the same as having a pivot in every
        row. So for a square matrix, the transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is
        one-to-one if and only if it is onto. For non-square matrices the two
        properties are independent <mdash/> except that, by
        <xref ref="rem-wide-never-injective" text="type-global"/> and
        <xref ref="rem-tall-never-surjective" text="type-global"/>, a wide matrix is
        never one-to-one and a tall matrix is never onto.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>
</section>
